{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>לִרְחֹץ עַל הַיְאוֹר</b> — Emend the verse and interpret it: \"And the daughter of Pharaoh went down עַל הַיְאוֹר to bathe in it\" — this is the language of Rabbi Solomon. If so, <b>עַל הַיְאוֹר</b> is like \"אֶל הַיְאוֹר,\" and likewise \"וַיֵּלֶךְ אֶלְקָנָה הָרָמָתָה עַל בֵּיתוֹ\" (I Samuel 2:11), \"כֹּה תֹאמְרוּ אִישׁ עַל רֵעֵהוּ וְאִישׁ אֶל אָחִיו\" (Jeremiah 23:35). It is also possible that there were steps in the Nile, and she went down from the king's palace to bathe at the first step that was in the Nile — she would not enter the current of the river — and she saw <b>the ark among the reeds</b> far from her, <b>and she sent her maidservant and took it</b>. Or it is like \"to bathe in the Nile,\" and similarly \"וְנָתַתָּ אוֹתָם עַל סַל אֶחָד\" (Exodus 29:3)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Rabbi Solomon = Rashi. The phrase לִרְחֹץ עַל הַיְאוֹר is emended by inserting the words 'in it' (בּוֹ) after לִרְחֹץ, per Rashi's reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:08.913905209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}